  "briefing": {
    "headline": "Offering home working on Wednesdays and Fridays is predicted to cut departures over the next 12 months by about a third, from 696 to 461 people (-34%, roughly 235 fewer leavers), with strongly positive sentiment (+1.23 on a -2 to +2 scale).",
    "most_at_risk": "No group reacts badly; the biggest winners are long commuters (-39% leaving vs -28% for short commuters) because they get back up to three hours on each home day, while gender, level, manager status and caregiving make little difference (-32% to -36%).",
    "risks": [
      "The size of the effect is the least reliable part: it rests on 48 modelled voices that are uniformly positive and similarly worded, so real attrition may fall by noticeably less than 34% even if the direction is right.",
      "The promise that promotion is unaffected may not hold in practice. If managers informally favour people they see in the office, the goodwill could turn into distrust, especially among long commuters and caregivers who use the home days most.",
      "Some short-commute, live-alone staff say they may miss having people around and will come in anyway. A half-empty office on Wednesdays and Fridays could weaken team contact for them, and fixed Friday home days may be read as a soft long weekend by people who doubt the policy."
    ],
    "design_changes": [
      "Put the policy in writing as an entitlement, not a manager favour, and brief managers before launch that home days must not count against anyone in reviews or promotions. Then check after the first review cycle that ratings and promotions do not differ between people who use the days and people who don't.",
      "Keep the office fully open and staffed on Wednesdays and Fridays, and say so clearly, so people who prefer the office (often short commuters and those living alone) can keep coming in without it looking unusual.",
      "Launch it as a policy you intend to keep, but measure it like a pilot. Track actual resignations and engagement by commute length over 6 to 12 months against the 696-leaver baseline, and commit publicly that any future change will come with notice, because withdrawing a benefit people have planned their lives around would likely cost more than never offering it."
    ],
    "confidence": "medium, because the direction is consistent across every group and matches the obvious logic of long Shanghai commutes, but the exact numbers come from a small, uniformly positive set of modelled voices with no dissent, so treat -34% as an upper-end estimate rather than a forecast."
  },
